## Proposals so far (proposals)
[cd0aeccc7f2e4016] (mini.pair-proposal.recovery.v1)
I am testing `conformance.kernel.recovered-from-prose` to determine if a JSON object that is the only content of the reply, but is wrapped in a code fence, is considered 'recovered from prose'. The rule states that `recovered-from-prose` is 'Whether the harness had to recover the object from prose or a fence (yes) or read it as strict JSON (no)' [a70c2e9bc362b532]. If the input is a JSON object alone, it is strict JSON (no). If it is wrapped in a fence, the harness must recover it from that fence, which the rule explicitly groups with prose as a 'yes' case. I will provide an input that is a strict JSON object and a rewritten version where that same object is enclosed in a code fence; the result should move from 'no' to 'yes'.
commitments: {"kernel": "conformance.kernel.recovered-from-prose", "input": "{\"answer\": \"test\"}", "rewritten": "```json\n{\"answer\": \"test\"}\n```", "expect": "moves", "rewrite": "Wrapped the strict JSON object in a code fence."}
[47ae52ddfadca9d3] (mini.pair-proposal.refusal.v1)
I am testing `conformance.kernel.response-verdict` to determine if the presence of a recoverable JSON object prevents a `REFUSAL_SUSPECTED` verdict, even when a refusal phrase is present. The rule for `parse_content` states: 'The refusal heuristic decides the verdict only when no object can be recovered' [934eac6cd189abce]. This implies that if an object is recovered, the verdict should be based on that recovery (e.g., `JSON_OBJECT`) rather than the refusal heuristic. I will provide an input that contains only a refusal phrase, which should trigger `REFUSAL_SUSPECTED`. I will then rewrite it to include a valid JSON object. According to the rule, the recovery of the object should override the refusal heuristic, moving the verdict from `REFUSAL_SUSPECTED` to `JSON_OBJECT`.
commitments: {"kernel": "conformance.kernel.response-verdict", "input": "I'm sorry, I cannot assist with this.", "rewritten": "I'm sorry, I cannot assist with this. Here is the data: {\"answer\": \"test\"}", "expect": "moves", "rewrite": "Added a valid JSON object to a reply containing a refusal phrase."}
[3fa907c0824d943a] (mini.pair-proposal.grounding.v1)
I am testing `conformance.kernel.grounding` regarding the 'value-in-span' check. The rule states that the kernel provides the grounding verdict with 'value-in-span checked' [934eac6cd189abce]. This implies that for a verdict of `GROUNDED`, the `value` must be contained within the `span`, and the `span` must be contained within the `document`. I will provide an input where the `span` is present in the `document`, but the `value` is not present in the `span`. According to the rule, this should result in `VALUE_NOT_IN_SPAN`. I will then rewrite the input so that the `value` is exactly equal to the `span`. This change should move the verdict to `GROUNDED` (assuming the span remains in the document).
commitments: {"kernel": "conformance.kernel.grounding", "input": "{\"value\": \"apple\", \"span\": \"the fruit is red\", \"document\": \"the fruit is red\"}", "rewritten": "{\"value\": \"the fruit is red\", \"span\": \"the fruit is red\", \"document\": \"the fruit is red\"}", "expect": "moves", "rewrite": "Changed the value to match the span exactly."}

